<BaseLayout title="About | Xiao Wang" description="Meet Xiao Wang: analytics leader at Musket, software engineer, dad to two boys, and a Boston-to-Houston road-trip veteran." pageClass="about-page">
  <section class="about-hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">A little about me</p><h1>Curious by nature.<br /><span class="accent">An engineer<br />at heart.</span></h1><p class="lead">I’m Xiao Wang, an analytics and technology leader based in Houston. I build systems that connect quantitative research with the people who use it.</p></div><img class="about-portrait" src="/images/xwang.jpg" alt="Xiao Wang" width="1195" height="943" /></section>
  <section class="section bio-section" data-reveal><p class="eyebrow">My background</p><div><h2>Beijing. Chicago.<br />Boston. Houston.</h2><p>I studied software engineering at Beijing University of Technology, then earned a master’s in computer science at the University of Illinois at Chicago. My early engineering work included Motorola and research software in Chicago.</p><p>In the Boston area, my focus moved toward quantitative research and trading technology. At Nautilus, I’ve worked on research platforms and technology strategy as Partner and CTO. At Fox Capital, I developed systems for strategy testing, trading, and risk management.</p><p>I joined Musket in October 2024 as a Quant Analyst and became Manager of Analytics in June 2025. Today, I lead work across quantitative modeling, data engineering, and web applications, partnering with trading teams to solve business problems.</p><a class="text-link" href="/cv/">Read my CV <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></section>
  <section class="section bio-section" data-reveal><p class="eyebrow">Life outside work</p><div><h2>A full house.<br />A new hometown.</h2><p>I’m a dad to two boys, Andrey and Eddie, and we have a family dog. In August 2025, I moved from the Boston area to Houston.</p><p>The move came with a memorable road trip: I drove from Boston to Houston with both boys and our dog. Planning that journey called for a different set of problem-solving skills than building an analytics platform.</p><p>I like the connection between those two parts of life: figuring things out, staying curious, and making room for whatever comes next.</p></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
